John Edward: Linguage skills are of great importance for citizens of the world
Scotland's independent schools have an academic-achieving history, resulting in an extraordindentary test results supported by sports, arts, music and other non-governmental studies.
With more than 30,000 disciples in Scotland and are represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), they work to provide students and their parents at the best level.
Independent schools aim to preparulate their students for their later education and higher education, the career they have chosen and to be a citizen of the world.
As educational institutions that can prepare a special curriculum for students and apply this curricula, modern languages continue to be a popular and necessity course in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak the language he understands to someone, in his mind,
if you speak native language, you appeal to your heart."
If we want to build relationships with people from other countries and establish mutual trust, it is a powerful reminder that we shouldn't rely solely on English.
On the basis of this year's final exam results, we can see that language classes in independent schools are at the top of the score charts with the highest passing grade rates.
During this year, a total of 68% of the students who took foreign language courses passed their courses with A, which is a high grade.
According to data collected from SCIS's 74-affiliated schools, 72 per cent of students who took Chinese classes, 72 per cent of German recipients, 69 per cent of French fields and 63 per per cent of those who took Spanish with A.
This data shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign-language courses as the basic skills that children and young people will definitely need in the future.
Elective language courses are now synonymous with FeTeMM (Science Sciences, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics) courses in the curricula of independent schools and other schools.
A study conducted by the United Kingdom Employment and Skills Commission in 2014 found that 17% of the reasons why employers are troubled when recruiting new jobs in vacant positions are associated with deficiencies in language skills.
For this reason, language abilities become even more obligatory to prepare young people for their future career.
These skills have been the main skills in the globalized world because more employers require licence for possible job opportunities.
If the person speaks a second language regardless of his/moving career, he/she will gain a real advantage in the future because he/she has the ability to use for a lifetime.
Being able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries takes a spontaneous presumption in the competition.
According to a survey conducted by YouGov in 2013, more than more than 4,000 people living in the United Kingdom don't have enough foreign languages to be able to chat, except for French (15%), the rate of language-speaking in any foreign language does not reach a two-digit number.
Therefore, investing in linguistic learning from today is of great importance for the children of the future.
Being able to speak more than one language, especially the languages of emerging economies, will increase children's chances of finding a suitable and affordable job in the future.
Languages that are taught in schools in Scotland varied.
Certain schools offer more traditional modern language options, while some schools also teach languages that are thought to be important for the United Kingdom in 2020, such as Chinese or Japanese.
Regardless of what area your child is interested, he/she can choose one of the various foreign language options that are taught by teachers who are experts in this field in independent schools.
Scottish-independent schools aim to provide a teaching environment that will prepare children for the future and equip them with the necessary skills for success.
In today's global business environment, foreign languages continue to be of vital importance for the future of the country.
In fact, modern languages should be viewed as "international communication skills."
Independent schools will continue to offer this option, diversity and success to Scotland's young people.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Scottish Board of Independent Schools
