HIV/Aids: China reported a 14% increase in the number of new cases
China announced a 14 per cent increase in the number of citizens with HIV and Aids.
According to health officials, the virus has influenced more than 280,000 people in the country.
More than 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
Contrary to past cases, it has been noted that most new cases have been transmitted through sexual intercourse.
In the past, the HIV virus was rapidly disseminated as a result of infected blood translating in some parts of China.
China's health officials, who made statements at a conference in Yunnan's province, however, said the number of people suffering from the HIV virus by blood translating has dropped to almost zero.
However, the number of people living with HIV and Aids in China has an increase of 100,000 people every year.
The HIV Virus transmitted by sexual intercourse poses a serious problem for the LGBT community in China.
Anti-excregarde against LGBT individuals is said to have continued discrimination in China, although it has been discontinued as a crime in 1997.
The study is estimated that between 70% and 90% of men who have sexual intercourse with men married women due to the country's conservative values.
The disease is often transmitted due to the lack of adequate attention to protection during these homosexual relationships.
The Chinese government has pledged to provide general access to HIV drugs as part of anti-discrimination efforts since 2003.
