Palu is located at the end of a narrow gulf on the western coast of Sulawesi Island, estimated to have a population of 379,800 in 2017.
When an earthquake and tsunami occurred, the city celebrated its 40th Anniversary.
Donggala has more than 300 km (180 miles) along the coastline northwest of Sulawesi.
It is estimated to have a population of around 299,200 perpetuation in 2017.
Fisheries and cultivation are the main livelihoods of the Central Sulawesi province, especially Donggala coastal region.
Nickel mining in the province is also important, but the mining is concentrated in Morowali, on the opposite bank of Sulawesi.
According to the Indonesian Anti-Disaster Agency, over the past 100 years, there have been several tsunami catastrophes in Palu and Donggala.
In 1938 a tsunami killed more than 200 people in Donggala, destroying hundreds of houses.
Another tsunami killed nine people in Donggala in 1996.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Fire Circle, which is seismically active, experiencing earthquakes on a regular basis.
Here are some serious earthquake and tsunami catastrophes in recent years:
2004: On December 26, a severe earthquake on the western coast of Aceh province in North Sumatra in Indonesia caused a tsunami that hit 14 countries, killing more than 216,000 people in Aceh.
In the end of March and in early April, severe earthquakes hit the western coast of Sumatra.
Hundreds of people have lost their lives on Nias Island off the coast of Sumatra.
The 6.8 magnitutude earthquake that hit Indonesia's most populous island Java triggered a tsunami, causing the island to hit the southern coast, killing about 700.
A 7.6 magnitude earthquake occurred near Padang, the capital of West Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people lost their lives.
In 2010: The 7.5 magnitutude earthquake off the Sumatra struck one of the Mentawai islands, resulting in a tsunami approaching 10 meters, resulting dozens of villages being destroyed and about 300 people were killed.
2016: A shallow magnitude earthquake in Aceh's Pidie Jaya ritual caused devastation and destruction and panic by causing the fatal damage to the earthquake and tsunami in 2004.
A tsunami was unsuccessful this time, but more than 100 people were killed due to demolished buildings.
2018: Violent quarries on Indonesia's tourist Lombok island have killed 500 people, mostly located north of the island.
