"Syrian asylum seekers' 'prepared' to return," Foreign Minister said
Syria says asylum seekers are ready to voluntarily and seek help to re-establish a country devastated by more than a seven-year-old war.
Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem told the United Nations General Assembly that the situation in the country has recovered.
"By the fight against terrorism, the ground is now more robust and our land is safer," he said.
The government continues its rehabilitation process to normalize areas destroyed by terrorists.
Due to unimanicial economic measures targeting their daily lives and livelihoods, all the conditions for voluntary responsident for migrants are forced to leave.
The UN estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country due to the war that started in 2011.
More than six million people still living in the country need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime is open to future assistance to rebuild the devastated country.
But he underlined that they would disregard probabilities and assistance from countries that sponsor the rebellion.
Europe embracing Ryder Cup victories in Paris
The European Team won the 2018 Ryder Cup, defeating the U.S. team at Le Golf National, out of France.
The U.S. suffered a sixth consecutive defeat in Europe and had not won any Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
