Red Tide News: Groups in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota are shrinking
The latest report from the Florida Fish and Natural Life Commission (FWC) shows a general downsizing of Red tidal groups in some parts of Tampa Bay.
According to FWC, unsuitable reproducation conditions have been reported from Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, which explains shrinking groups.
During a proliferation period, the red tide expands by 130 miles from North Pinellas to South Lee.
Smaller groups can be located about 10 miles off the Hillsborough Territory, but fewer compared to last week.
The red tide has also been observed in Pasco District.
Last week, mid-sized groups were observed on the shores and off the coasts of the Pinellas Territory, on the offshore and off the Hillsborough Territory, with certain ambiguous and large groups in the Manatee Region, on the shores and offshores of the Sarasota Region, in certain uncertain and medium-sized groups in the Charlotte Region, certain obscure and large groups were observed on the shores and offshore of the Lee Zone, and in the Collier Region of small groups.
